The universe is essentially just a collection of solid stuff.

03:42.860 --> 03:45.640
And on the complete opposite end of the spectrum, you have Heraclitus.

03:45.960 --> 03:48.340
He is the original becoming philosopher.

03:48.600 --> 03:49.200
The river guy.

03:49.260 --> 03:49.820
The river guy.

03:49.940 --> 03:50.100
Yeah.

03:50.140 --> 03:55.200
He argued for process ontology. This gives us the famous idea that you can never step in the same river twice.

03:55.320 --> 03:56.800
Because the water is always moving.

03:57.100 --> 04:02.380
Exactly. For Heraclitus, the fundamental stuff of reality isn't solid stuff at all.

04:02.380 --> 04:05.860
It's fire. It's flux. It's constant transformation.

04:06.380 --> 04:08.320
And that idea didn't just stay with the Greeks, right?

04:08.320 --> 04:09.040
No, not at all.

04:09.320 --> 04:18.000
Fast forward to the 20th century, and you have thinkers like Alfred North Whitehead building an entire systematic metaphysics out of this concept.

04:18.340 --> 04:21.580
Whitehead basically said the universe isn't made of enduring substances, right?

04:21.700 --> 04:25.980
Right. He said it's made of momentary events, which he called occasions of experience.

04:25.980 --> 04:32.820
So the things we think of as solid objects, like a rock or a table, are actually just temporary patterns.

04:33.040 --> 04:34.520
Like a whirlpool in a river or something.

04:34.660 --> 04:39.640
Exactly like that. They persist by repeating themselves over and over in the constant flow of time.

04:39.840 --> 04:41.680
So it's nouns versus verbs.

04:41.860 --> 04:42.040
Yep.

04:42.040 --> 04:52.160
The universe as a giant box of static Lego bricks versus the universe as a constantly flowing waterfall where the water just, you know, temporarily looks like a brick.

04:52.440 --> 04:54.540
It's a debate that has raged for millennia.

04:54.540 --> 05:00.860
But the author of our source essay steps back and points out a massive blind spot that both sides actually share.

05:01.140 --> 05:01.780
A blind spot.

05:02.100 --> 05:09.560
Yeah, because whether you think reality is made of solid things or flowing events, you are still inherently asking what there is.

05:09.680 --> 05:11.260
Oh, I see. You're looking for a noun.

05:11.260 --> 05:14.760
Right. You are looking for a definitive entity to point to.

05:15.240 --> 05:22.260
But the essay proposes a radical third way, which it calls accessibility ontology or a constraint first ontology.

05:23.020 --> 05:25.880
Okay. This is where we get back to waking up in that pitch dark room.

05:25.920 --> 05:26.180
Yeah.

05:26.240 --> 05:31.160
Because this theory says the fundamental stratum of reality is neither a thing nor an event.

05:31.300 --> 05:31.620
No.

05:31.980 --> 05:33.000
It is a geometry.

05:33.000 --> 05:41.540
It is the invisible structure of constraints that dictates which configurations are accessible from whatever state you are currently in.

05:42.220 --> 05:45.860
The math in the paper represents this as a sort of master map of possibilities.

05:46.200 --> 05:46.320
Okay.

05:46.400 --> 05:54.780
It's a function that looks at exactly how things are right now and defines the exact set of other configurations that are immediately accessible in the next instant.

05:55.120 --> 05:58.000
It's the literal map of what is allowed to happen next.

05:58.000 --> 05:59.820
Okay. I have to stop you there and push back a little.

05:59.860 --> 06:00.360
Sure. Go ahead.

06:00.640 --> 06:02.620
Because that sounds incredibly abstract.

06:03.340 --> 06:11.560
If reality is just this invisible web of constraints, like a map of what can happen, where do the objects and the events actually come from?

06:11.740 --> 06:13.480
That's a million-dollar question.

06:13.480 --> 06:15.480
Because I am holding a coffee mug right now.

06:15.920 --> 06:17.380
It feels pretty solid to me.

06:17.520 --> 06:18.360
It has weight.

06:18.580 --> 06:19.220
It's a noun.

06:19.680 --> 06:22.620
It doesn't feel like an accessible configuration, you know?

06:22.720 --> 06:22.980
Right.

06:23.080 --> 06:25.860
And that is the genius of how this ontology handles depth.

06:26.220 --> 06:30.660
The framework argues that objects and processes are emergent properties of the constraints.

06:30.820 --> 06:32.020
They bubble up from it.

06:32.120 --> 06:33.140
Bubble up from the constraints.

06:33.240 --> 06:33.400
Yeah.

06:33.780 --> 06:39.340
An object like your coffee mug is defined in this theory simply as a highly constrained basin.

06:39.780 --> 06:40.780
It's an attractor.

06:40.880 --> 06:41.580
An attractor.

06:41.800 --> 06:42.500
Like a magnet.

06:42.500 --> 06:43.340
More like a valley.

06:43.980 --> 06:50.060
Imagine a region in the landscape of possibilities where the options loop back on themselves so tightly that a pattern just gets trapped.

06:50.580 --> 06:51.400
It persists.

06:51.520 --> 06:52.520
Okay. I think I'm falling.

06:53.020 --> 06:57.480
Your coffee mug is just a localized loop of highly probable states holding a shape.

06:57.860 --> 07:01.200
Basically because the constraints won't allow the atoms to easily go anywhere else.

07:01.620 --> 07:02.420
And a process.

07:02.760 --> 07:05.880
A process is just the trajectory taken through the field.

07:06.220 --> 07:09.520
It's the sequence of moving from one accessible state to the next.

07:09.520 --> 07:14.240
You know, I was trying to wrap my head around this last night, and I want to try an analogy on you to see if this tracks.

07:14.360 --> 07:14.860
Let's hear it.

07:14.920 --> 07:17.440
Let's say reality is a video game.

07:17.540 --> 07:18.380
Okay. A video game.

07:18.380 --> 07:29.760
If you believe in substance ontology, the Greek idea of being, you think the most fundamental part of the game is the 3D character model, like the armor, the weapons, the textures painted on the hero.

07:29.900 --> 07:30.660
Visual stuff.

07:30.660 --> 07:39.640
Right. And if you believe in process ontology, the becoming side, you think the most fundamental part is the running, the jumping, the animations, the frame rate.

07:39.660 --> 07:40.220
The action.

07:40.480 --> 07:41.100
Yeah, the action.

07:41.160 --> 07:41.360
Right.

07:41.720 --> 07:44.600
But accessibility ontology says you're both wrong.

07:45.240 --> 07:49.960
The most fundamental reality of the video game is the collision mesh.

07:50.500 --> 07:52.300
The collision mesh? Let's explore that.

07:52.300 --> 07:56.880
Yeah. It's the invisible geometry that the developers build underneath the world that you never actually see.

07:57.160 --> 08:00.940
It's the invisible walls, the solid floors, the hitboxes around enemies.

08:01.100 --> 08:03.060
The rules of the physical space.

08:03.600 --> 08:03.980
Exactly.

08:04.800 --> 08:10.180
The collision mesh dictates where the character model can stand and where the running animation can go.

08:10.860 --> 08:16.440
You don't see the constraints, but they completely govern every object and every event in the game.

08:16.560 --> 08:17.560
That is spot on.

08:17.560 --> 08:25.260
In fact, gamers only ever notice the collision mesh when it breaks, like when their character clips through a wall and falls into endless gray space.

08:25.360 --> 08:32.240
That is a phenomenal way to visualize it, and it aligns beautifully with a concept in the philosophy of science called structural realism.

08:32.460 --> 08:33.300
Structural realism.

08:33.400 --> 08:38.180
Yeah. Structural realism argues that we don't actually need underlying stuff to build a universe.

08:38.360 --> 08:39.360
We don't need stuff.

08:39.460 --> 08:42.940
No, the pure relational geometry of possibility is enough.

08:43.100 --> 08:43.540
Wow.

08:43.540 --> 08:52.440
Think about your video game analogy. The invisible collision mesh determines every path a character can take, and it determines every corner where a character might get stuck.

08:52.580 --> 08:52.860
Right.

08:53.180 --> 09:02.960
But no single path you run and no single corner you stand in can fully map the entire massive invisible field of what was possible in the game.

09:03.320 --> 09:06.140
The constraint field is deeper. It comes first.

09:06.140 --> 09:15.660
Okay, so if the universe is fundamentally this invisible collision mesh of constraints, what happens when human beings try to interact with it?

09:15.780 --> 09:17.320
That is where it gets really tricky.

09:17.460 --> 09:27.860
Because, I mean, when we try to observe it or map it or even just remember what happened yesterday, my brain is definitely not tracking the entire invisible geometry of the universe.

09:27.860 --> 09:33.120
No, it isn't, and it can't. And that brings us to the mathematical brick wall that the essay explores.

09:33.280 --> 09:34.060
The brick wall.

09:34.080 --> 09:40.980
A huge one. Whenever we interact with this infinitely rich foundational constraint field, we are forced to compress it.

09:41.340 --> 09:44.560
The essay refers to this process as projection.

09:44.800 --> 09:47.920
Projection, right. The classic map versus territory problem.

09:48.020 --> 09:48.400
Exactly.

09:48.600 --> 09:56.780
If the universe is this massive, unimaginably rich space of trajectories, our brains and our sciences have to shrink it down just to be able to hold it in our heads.

09:56.780 --> 10:01.140
The source material maps this out very rigorously. It basically sets up two realms.

10:01.500 --> 10:06.440
First, you have the completely rich, uncompressed reality of all the trajectories and all the constraints.

10:06.640 --> 10:07.300
Real territory.

10:07.480 --> 10:08.840
The real territory, yes.

10:09.440 --> 10:12.220
Then you have the coarser observable space.

10:12.960 --> 10:18.560
This is the map we make of the territory. This is what we can actually see, measure, and talk about.

10:18.560 --> 10:30.720
And the essay proves that if your rich reality is vastly larger than your observable map, which it obviously is, because the universe is infinitely more complex than a human thought.

10:30.820 --> 10:31.260
Obviously.

10:31.740 --> 10:38.960
Then information must be lost. Like, it's a mathematical certainty. You cannot compress reality without dropping data on the floor.

10:38.960 --> 10:45.340
Right. And the specific data that gets dropped, the stuff we lose, is what the author calls the fiber, or the fiber coordinate.

10:45.580 --> 10:46.800
The fiber. Okay, what is that?

10:46.820 --> 10:54.260
Let's walk through a thought experiment. Let's say you have two completely different, incredibly complex historical paths in the rich reality.

10:54.440 --> 10:54.780
Okay.

10:54.780 --> 11:00.360
But when they are compressed into our observable world, they both end up producing the exact same final result.

11:00.480 --> 11:01.860
So they look identical on our map.

11:02.060 --> 11:11.120
Exactly. Because they map to the exact same outcome, those two different histories become completely indistinguishable to anyone who only has access to the map.

11:11.440 --> 11:16.760
The fiber is the set of all those hidden alternate paths that look identical on the surface.

11:17.060 --> 11:22.380
I love that word fiber. It makes me think of like a bundle of wires behind a TV.

11:22.720 --> 11:23.860
That's a good way to look at it.

11:23.860 --> 11:26.540
I think of this like taking a photograph of my coffee mug.

11:26.900 --> 11:30.460
The actual 3D coffee mug in my hand is the rich space.

11:31.240 --> 11:35.820
The flat 2D photograph printed on a piece of paper is the observable space.

11:35.880 --> 11:36.760
Right. It's compressed.

11:37.020 --> 11:42.480
When you project a 3D mug onto a flat 2D photo, you inherently lose the depth.

11:42.860 --> 11:45.340
That missing dimension of depth is the fiber coordinate.

11:45.460 --> 11:46.780
Exactly. You lost the data.

11:46.780 --> 11:54.160
And because you lost it, a photo of a real coffee mug and a photo of a cardboard cutout of a coffee mug might look exactly the same.

11:54.840 --> 12:00.040
Multiple rich, complex realities map to the identical observable outcome.

12:00.180 --> 12:02.380
That is a perfect, tangible visual.

12:02.380 --> 12:07.340
Now, if we take your photograph analogy and apply it to physics, things get incredibly wild.

12:07.520 --> 12:08.140
Oh boy. Physics.

12:08.320 --> 12:09.380
Don't worry. We'll keep it grounded.

12:09.840 --> 12:18.320
The essay takes this concept of lost fiber coordinates and uses it to completely redefine thermodynamics, specifically the second law of thermodynamics.

12:18.320 --> 12:21.520
Okay. High school physics class flashback.

12:21.920 --> 12:29.200
The second law of thermodynamics is the one that says entropy or disorder always increases over time in a closed system, right?

12:29.340 --> 12:30.480
Yes. That's the one.

12:30.540 --> 12:37.800
The classic example my teacher gave was that a shattered glass doesn't magically reassemble itself and a messy teenage bedroom doesn't clean itself.

12:38.060 --> 12:39.140
Classic examples.

12:39.600 --> 12:43.420
We are taught that entropy is just a brute, unavoidable fact of physics.

12:44.080 --> 12:46.600
The universe inherently prefers disorder.

12:46.600 --> 12:50.760
And the author of our source essay says that interpretation is fundamentally backwards.

12:51.120 --> 12:52.240
Backwards? Wait, really?

12:52.320 --> 12:57.600
Yes. The essay argues that standard thermodynamic entropy, which was formalized by Ludwig Boltzmann,

12:58.080 --> 13:03.820
is actually just a special, narrow case of a much broader mathematical concept called projection entropy.

13:04.060 --> 13:05.980
Okay. I'm going to need you to hold my hand here.

13:06.080 --> 13:08.700
What is projection entropy in plain English?

13:09.300 --> 13:13.540
It means that the second law of thermodynamics isn't about physical disorder at all.

13:13.620 --> 13:14.100
It's not.

13:14.100 --> 13:18.340
No. It is a structural necessity of how we project reality.

13:18.820 --> 13:20.500
Remember the lost fiber coordinates?

13:20.740 --> 13:22.040
The missing depth from the photograph.

13:22.400 --> 13:25.680
Exactly. If a system doesn't track those lost dimensions,

13:26.040 --> 13:30.300
if it operates entirely on the flattened 2D photograph instead of the 3D reality,

13:30.700 --> 13:32.260
it can't reverse the process.

13:32.400 --> 13:32.800
Oh, I see.

13:32.860 --> 13:33.560
It forgets.

13:33.920 --> 13:36.440
Entropy increases not because the universe loves chaos,

13:36.820 --> 13:41.580
but simply because you no longer have the geometric information required to go backward.

13:41.580 --> 13:43.100
You've lost the instructions.

13:43.360 --> 13:50.580
Wait, wait, wait. Are you saying that forgetting is quite literally just the expansion of these untrackable fibers?

13:50.860 --> 13:51.120
Yes.

13:51.300 --> 13:55.700
And remembering is simply the preservation of constraints so you don't lose the coordinates?

13:55.860 --> 13:56.380
Precisely.

13:56.780 --> 13:58.280
Forgetting is fiber expansion.

13:58.780 --> 14:01.800
Remembering is the active preservation of discriminating constraints.

14:01.800 --> 14:03.180
That is mind-blowing.

14:03.260 --> 14:07.460
If you maintain the constraints, you don't lose the depth, and entropy doesn't increase for you.

14:07.920 --> 14:11.940
The essay provides a brilliant non-physics example of this to really ground it.

14:12.040 --> 14:13.620
It calls it semantic entropy.

14:13.800 --> 14:16.820
Semantic entropy, like the entropy of meaning in a conversation.

14:17.180 --> 14:17.640
Exactly.

14:18.200 --> 14:20.540
Think about having a conversation with a close friend.

14:20.540 --> 14:28.080
The rich, uncompressed space is the entire history of everything you and your friend have ever said to each other.

14:28.180 --> 14:30.280
Every shared experience, every inside joke.

14:30.380 --> 14:31.800
A huge amount of data.

14:32.040 --> 14:38.120
And the observable space is the semantic meaning of the one single sentence you are speaking right now.

14:38.600 --> 14:39.720
Just the words in the air.

14:39.860 --> 14:40.180
Right.

14:40.700 --> 14:48.060
High semantic entropy means there are thousands of different past conversations that could have logically led to this exact same sentence.

14:48.160 --> 14:49.720
Oh, I have the perfect example for this.

14:49.720 --> 14:49.980
Yeah.

14:50.200 --> 14:52.680
The four most terrifying words in the English language.

14:52.760 --> 14:53.300
What's that?

14:53.440 --> 14:54.300
We need to talk.

14:54.580 --> 14:55.560
Oh, wow.

14:56.100 --> 14:59.100
Yes, a masterpiece of semantic entropy.

14:59.400 --> 14:59.660
Right.

14:59.760 --> 15:03.180
If someone walks up to you and says, we need to talk, the entropy is massive.

15:03.280 --> 15:05.000
The fiber of possibilities is huge.

15:05.060 --> 15:06.400
You have no idea what it means.

15:06.460 --> 15:06.820
Exactly.

15:06.980 --> 15:07.120
Yeah.

15:07.220 --> 15:07.900
Are you getting fired?

15:08.380 --> 15:09.460
Are you being broken up with?

15:09.920 --> 15:11.480
Do they just find out a secret about you?

15:11.480 --> 15:13.880
Do they just watch a weird documentary and want to discuss it?

15:14.240 --> 15:19.480
Many completely different rich histories project down into that exact same observable sentence.

15:19.720 --> 15:23.720
You have lost the coordinates to know which history you are currently in.

15:24.300 --> 15:30.640
But if they walked up and said, we need to talk about the documentary we watched last night, the semantic entropy drops.

15:31.100 --> 15:31.620
Significantly.

15:31.780 --> 15:32.860
The constraints are tighter.

15:32.860 --> 15:39.140
The path that produced the statement is obvious and recoverable just from the meaning alone.

15:39.300 --> 15:39.560
Yes.

15:39.900 --> 15:46.900
And the essay also extends this to something called institutional entropy, which is an incredible way to look at human civilization.

15:47.320 --> 15:48.020
